        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(agents): preserve ANSI sanitizer state across bash chunks",
        "body": "Closes #103668\n\n## What Problem This Solves\n\nWhen a bash process splits ANSI/OSC controls across stdout callbacks, per-chunk stateless stripping can leak control-sequence fragments into streamed tool output, final results, and spill files.\n\nThis adds a reusable incremental ANSI stripper to terminal-core and keeps one sanitizer alive for each bash command. One-shot stripping is unchanged while streamed sanitization becomes callback-boundary invariant.\n\n## Evidence\n\nExact rebased head: `2fdef23cef05af222ade85108301e0023d52d33f`\n\n- Focused Vitest passed: 3 files / 51 tests across 2 shards.\n- Direct `oxlint` and `oxfmt --check --threads=1` passed for every changed file.\n- `git diff --check upstream/main...HEAD` passed.\n- Real `executeBashWithOperations()` child-process coverage verifies clean split streamed chunks, final `ABCDEFGH`, and sanitized spill-file output.\n\nThe aggregate `run-oxlint.mjs` preparation step is currently blocked by an unrelated current-main `packages/net-policy/src/ip.ts` type-boundary failure; direct changed-file lint passed.\n",
        "branch": "fix/103668-stateful-ansi-sanitizer",
